24
00:00:58,535 --> 00:01:01,820
So, how do you go about
writing that email that

25
00:01:01,820 --> 00:01:03,770
effectively influences
your audience

26
00:01:03,770 --> 00:01:05,495
to consider your idea?

27
00:01:05,495 --> 00:01:08,180
Well, it turns out
there are four tried

28
00:01:08,180 --> 00:01:10,850
and true steps of
effective influencing.

29
00:01:10,850 --> 00:01:14,450
Leadership expert, Dr.
Jay A Conger identifies

30
00:01:14,450 --> 00:01:16,685
the steps to
effective influencing

31
00:01:16,685 --> 00:01:18,820
as: establish credibility,

32
00:01:18,820 --> 00:01:20,420
frame for common ground,

33
00:01:20,420 --> 00:01:23,630
provide evidence and
connect emotionally.

34
00:01:23,630 --> 00:01:25,935
Let's discuss each step.

35
00:01:25,935 --> 00:01:27,230
The first step to

36
00:01:27,230 --> 00:01:30,775
effective influencing is
to establish credibility.

37
00:01:30,775 --> 00:01:33,230
During this step,
you make the case

38
00:01:33,230 --> 00:01:35,615
for why your audience
should listen to you.

39
00:01:35,615 --> 00:01:37,670
According to Dr. Conger,

40
00:01:37,670 --> 00:01:40,130
credibility comes
from two sources,

41
00:01:40,130 --> 00:01:43,015
expertise, and relationships.

42
00:01:43,015 --> 00:01:45,500
You need to demonstrate
to your audience

43
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:47,810
that you're an expert
on a given topic,

44
00:01:47,810 --> 00:01:49,895
whether that's through
professional experience,

45
00:01:49,895 --> 00:01:51,125
extensive research,

46
00:01:51,125 --> 00:01:53,900
or something else, and
you need to demonstrate

47
00:01:53,900 --> 00:01:55,490
relationship credibility by

48
00:01:55,490 --> 00:01:57,080
establishing that you're honest,

49
00:01:57,080 --> 00:02:00,460
trustworthy, and someone
who they want to work with.

50
00:02:00,460 --> 00:02:03,410
To establish credibility,
you might kick off

51
00:02:03,410 --> 00:02:04,730
your Office Green email by

52
00:02:04,730 --> 00:02:06,335
greeting the recipient by name,

53
00:02:06,335 --> 00:02:07,885
then writing something like,

54
00:02:07,885 --> 00:02:11,310
"I'm Elita, a lead project
manager at Office Green.

55
00:02:11,310 --> 00:02:14,890
Your colleague, Alex, passed
on your contact information.

56
00:02:14,890 --> 00:02:17,960
Alex and I worked together
to launch new services at

57
00:02:17,960 --> 00:02:21,020
Office Green before she
joined your organization."

58
00:02:21,020 --> 00:02:24,050
In these opening lines,
you've established expertise,

59
00:02:24,050 --> 00:02:25,820
credibility by
introducing your role

60
00:02:25,820 --> 00:02:26,990
at office green and by

61
00:02:26,990 --> 00:02:28,310
suddenly highlighting that you've

62
00:02:28,310 --> 00:02:30,370
launched new services
in the past.

63
00:02:30,370 --> 00:02:32,899
To demonstrate
relationship credibility,

64
00:02:32,899 --> 00:02:34,850
you've established that
you and the recipient

65
00:02:34,850 --> 00:02:36,950
have a shared contact
who has worked closely

66
00:02:36,950 --> 00:02:38,960
with you in the past
and can vouch for

67
00:02:38,960 --> 00:02:42,140
your trustworthiness and
emotional intelligence.

68
00:02:42,140 --> 00:02:45,280
The second step is to
frame for common ground.

69
00:02:45,280 --> 00:02:47,480
In this step, you'll
make the case

70
00:02:47,480 --> 00:02:50,270
for how your idea can
benefit your audience.

71
00:02:50,270 --> 00:02:52,370
To determine this, you'll need

72
00:02:52,370 --> 00:02:55,445
a strong understanding of your
audience and their values.

73
00:02:55,445 --> 00:02:58,900
In essence, what about your
idea will appeal to them,

74
00:02:58,900 --> 00:03:00,470
and how will they stand to

75
00:03:00,470 --> 00:03:03,600
benefit from agreeing
to your idea?

76
00:03:03,620 --> 00:03:06,080
In our Office Green email,

77
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:07,550
you might establish that you've

78
00:03:07,550 --> 00:03:09,620
researched their
organization extensively

79
00:03:09,620 --> 00:03:11,300
and believe that this new service

80
00:03:11,300 --> 00:03:13,325
might align with their
current offerings.

81
00:03:13,325 --> 00:03:16,390
For example, you might
write something like this,

82
00:03:16,390 --> 00:03:18,530
"We're launching a
service to provide

83
00:03:18,530 --> 00:03:21,230
top clients with desk
plants and we'd like to

84
00:03:21,230 --> 00:03:23,120
explore a bundle offering to pair

85
00:03:23,120 --> 00:03:25,720
high-quality chocolate
with each plant order.

86
00:03:25,720 --> 00:03:27,470
I've admired your organizations

87
00:03:27,470 --> 00:03:29,330
push in recent years to work with

88
00:03:29,330 --> 00:03:31,670
other lifestyle and
wellness brands and I

89
00:03:31,670 --> 00:03:33,320
think there may be
a great opportunity

90
00:03:33,320 --> 00:03:35,430
for us to collaborate."

91
00:03:35,630 --> 00:03:38,195
In this portion of the email,

92
00:03:38,195 --> 00:03:39,590
you've established
that you've done

93
00:03:39,590 --> 00:03:41,480
your research on the
organization and that

94
00:03:41,480 --> 00:03:43,340
their previous
partnerships indicate that

95
00:03:43,340 --> 00:03:45,790
working together
might be a great fit.

96
00:03:45,790 --> 00:03:48,480
The next step is to
provide evidence.

97
00:03:48,480 --> 00:03:50,750
In this step, you'll
make your case through

98
00:03:50,750 --> 00:03:53,365
hard data and persuasive
storytelling.

99
00:03:53,365 --> 00:03:55,610
Numbers aren't strong
enough on their own.

100
00:03:55,610 --> 00:03:57,940
They need stories
to liven them up.

101
00:03:57,940 --> 00:03:59,705
In our Office Green email,

102
00:03:59,705 --> 00:04:02,680
you might appeal to your
recipient with a line like,

103
00:04:02,680 --> 00:04:04,730
"We recently surveyed clients to

104
00:04:04,730 --> 00:04:06,560
gauge interest in this
kind of offering,

105
00:04:06,560 --> 00:04:09,680
and your brand came
up again and again."

106
00:04:09,680 --> 00:04:11,690
In this example, you

107
00:04:11,690 --> 00:04:13,520
provided evidence
through the results of

108
00:04:13,520 --> 00:04:15,020
client surveys which showed

109
00:04:15,020 --> 00:04:17,314
overwhelmingly positive
brand recognition

110
00:04:17,314 --> 00:04:19,040
for our shared audience.

111
00:04:19,040 --> 00:04:21,715
The last step is to
connect emotionally.

112
00:04:21,715 --> 00:04:23,360
In this step, you'll

113
00:04:23,360 --> 00:04:25,640
demonstrate to your audience
that you're emotionally

114
00:04:25,640 --> 00:04:27,560
committed to your
idea and you'll do

115
00:04:27,560 --> 00:04:30,155
your best to match
their emotional state.

116
00:04:30,155 --> 00:04:31,835
In our Office Green email,

117
00:04:31,835 --> 00:04:34,130
you might demonstrate
an emotional connection

118
00:04:34,130 --> 00:04:36,635
by tapping into
their brand ethos.

119
00:04:36,635 --> 00:04:39,620
For example, you might
write something like,

120
00:04:39,620 --> 00:04:41,270
"We've been following
your profile

121
00:04:41,270 --> 00:04:42,920
on Instagram and love your posts

122
00:04:42,920 --> 00:04:44,270
on chocolate's connection to

123
00:04:44,270 --> 00:04:46,820
living a well and
balanced lifestyle.

124
00:04:46,820 --> 00:04:48,770
Perhaps we can discuss combining

125
00:04:48,770 --> 00:04:50,990
forces to bring this
message to an even

126
00:04:50,990 --> 00:04:53,240
wider audience," and then

127
00:04:53,240 --> 00:04:55,880
you end your note with
a friendly closer,

128
00:04:55,880 --> 00:04:58,085
something like, "If
you're interested,

129
00:04:58,085 --> 00:04:59,600
I'd love to connect
and share what

130
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:01,940
our partner program
is all about."

131
00:05:01,940 --> 00:05:05,090
To recap, establishing
credibility,

132
00:05:05,090 --> 00:05:06,800
framing for common ground,

133
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:09,290
providing evidence and
connecting emotionally,

134
00:05:09,290 --> 00:05:11,450
are four proven, effective steps

135
00:05:11,450 --> 00:05:14,375
you can apply when attempting
to influence others.

136
00:05:14,375 --> 00:05:16,910
Dr. Conger also identified four

137
00:05:16,910 --> 00:05:18,620
common mistakes that people make

138
00:05:18,620 --> 00:05:20,720
when attempting to
influence others.

139
00:05:20,720 --> 00:05:23,615
They'll often approach their
audience too aggressively,

140
00:05:23,615 --> 00:05:26,975
and that tends to put
people off ideas entirely.

141
00:05:26,975 --> 00:05:29,435
They might also
resist compromise,

142
00:05:29,435 --> 00:05:32,585
which is crucial for any
kind of mutual agreement.

143
00:05:32,585 --> 00:05:35,510
They'll focus too much on
developing their argument for

144
00:05:35,510 --> 00:05:38,675
the idea and not enough time
establishing credibility,

145
00:05:38,675 --> 00:05:40,340
framing for common ground,

146
00:05:40,340 --> 00:05:42,095
providing evidence and connecting

147
00:05:42,095 --> 00:05:43,790
emotionally, and they'll

148
00:05:43,790 --> 00:05:45,560
assume that they can work out

149
00:05:45,560 --> 00:05:48,655
an agreement through
just one conversation.

150
00:05:48,655 --> 00:05:51,620
These mistakes can
jeopardize your attempts to

151
00:05:51,620 --> 00:05:53,390
influence others and limit

152
00:05:53,390 --> 00:05:55,790
your ability to
build relationships,

153
00:05:55,790 --> 00:05:58,970
so be mindful of these common
mistakes when preparing

154
00:05:58,970 --> 00:06:02,305
to pitch another person
on your big idea.

155
00:06:02,305 --> 00:06:05,935
Great job. Now you know the
